title: MondayClient1
position: 26,-44
---

/// Triggered by the player interacting with the client

Cliente1: Hola, quiero una hamburguesa con carne y queso, por favor.

/// Since the player is under the "irritabilidad" status effect, Unity must be prepared to ignore the line sent by Yarn Spinner when the player selects a non-mean option. This is done by putting a #choice tag at the end of every dialogue option, and telling Unity to ignore any lines that have that tag when the "irritabilidad" status effect is active.

/// The outcome of selecting a non-mean dialogue option can only happen if "irritabilidad" is not active, and if it does happen the node is interrupted after that, because the default flow is the consecuence of the mean option.

/// The player's mean line appears again after the options, since it must be sent in any case after the player's selection is ignored, and it is ignored if "irritabilidad" isn't active, for in that case selecting the mean option would already send the original line.

/// In order to make dialogue options bypass "irritabilidad" for narrative purposes, the #choice tag must simply not be included. The outcomes of non-mean options are shown in some nodes here to illustrate how it would be done in the rest of the game, but they were not written for the events of monday since getting "irritabilidad" is unavoidable that day.

-> Tomas: Sí señor, ya se la preparo. #choice
    <<if $hasIrritabilidad== false>>
        Cliente1: ¡Muchas gracias! #happy
        <<stop>>
    <<endif>>
-> Tomas: Agh, bueno. #angry #choice

Tomas: Agh, bueno. <<if $hasIrritabilidad== true>> #angry

Cliente1: Hm, estos jóvenes de hoy en día son muy groseros. #angry

<<set_order "Pan, Carne, Queso, Pan">>
===

